{"step_index":21,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-17T18:52:44Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":false,"Summary":"Comprehensive README.md operating manual for the Smart City Logistics Carpooling System C++ DSA Visualizer","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"# 🚗 Smart City Logistics – Carpooling System (C++ DSA Live Visualizer)\n\n> A modern, interactive Web Application designed with a dark-mode cyberpunk glassmorphism aesthetic, dynamic mouse-driven 3D anti-gravity tilt physics, and real-time visualization of C++ backend Data Structures & Algorithms.\n\n---\n\n## 📁 Package Contents\n\n```text\nsmart-city-carpool/\n├── index.html        # Main application UI & 3D canvas layouts\n├── styles.css        # Glassmorphic cyber theme & 3D anti-gravity tilt transforms\n├── app.js            # Virtual C++ data structures engine & canvas rendering\n└── README.md         # Full operating manual & feature guide\n```\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ How to Run & Operate the Application\n\n### Option 1: Direct Browser Launch (Simplest)\n1. Double-click `index.html` or right-click and choose **\"Open with\"** -> **Google Chrome**, **Microsoft Edge**, **Brave**, or **Mozilla Firefox**.\n2. No internet server, build tools, or compilers required! All libraries are bundled via standard CDNs.\n\n### Option 2: Local HTTP Server (VS Code Live Server or Python)\nIf you prefer running via a local development server:\n- **VS Code**: Right-click `index.html` and click **\"Open with Live Server\"**.\n- **Python**: Run `python -m http.server 8000` in the extracted folder and open `http://localhost:8000`.\n\n---\n\n## 🎮 How to Operate Each Feature & Module\n\n### 1. 🌟 One-Click Quickstart: \"Preset City Demo\"\n- In the top header bar, click the **\"Preset City Demo\"** button.\n- This immediately loads sample drivers, populates the incoming passenger queue, fills a circular waitlist slot, computes a sample shortest route on the 3D map, and matches an active ride.\n\n---\n\n### 2. 🗺️ 3D Spatial City Map & Dijkstra Shortest Path\n*Data Structures: Graph (Adjacency List) + Min-Heap Priority Queue (`std::priority_queue`)*\n\n- **Rotate the 3D City**: Click and hold anywhere on the city map canvas and drag your mouse. The city grid, elevated nodes, and laser routes will rotate in 3D spatial perspective.\n- **Zoom In / Out**: Use the mouse scroll wheel over the map canvas.\n- **Inspect Nodes**: Hover your mouse over any city hub (e.g. `Alpha Tech Core`, `Cyber Harbor`, `Neo Metro Center`) to view elevation and coordinates in the HUD overlay.\n- **Calculate Shortest Route**:\n  1. Pick a **Source** (e.g. `ALPHA`).\n  2. Pick a **Destination** (e.g. `HORIZON`).\n  3. Click **\"Compute Shortest Path\"**.\n  4. The optimal shortest route lights up with glowing cyan/emerald beams.\n  5. The **Min-Heap Step Tracer** below displays every relaxation step and extracted node distance in real time.\n\n---\n\n### 3. 👥 Passenger Queues (FIFO Pipeline & Circular Waiting Queue)\n*Data Structures: Dynamic Queue (FIFO) & Fixed Circular Array Buffer (Capacity 5)*\n\n- **Enqueue to Main Queue (FIFO)**:\n  1. Type a passenger name (or leave blank for an auto-generated citizen).\n  2. Choose their Pickup and Drop hubs.\n  3. Click **\"Enqueue (FIFO)\"**. The passenger appears at the end of the conveyor pipeline. The first item is marked with a glowing `FRONT (HEAD)` badge.\n- **Dequeue**: Click **\"Dequeue Front\"** to remove the first passenger in line (FIFO order).\n- **Circular Waitlist Buffer (Capacity 5)**:\n  - Click **\"Push Waitlist\"** to insert standby passengers into the circular ring array.\n  - Watch the pointers `F: [front] | R: [rear]` update modulo 5: `(front + count) % 5`.\n  - When all 5 slots are occupied, attempting to push will trigger an **OVERFLOW** warning.\n  - Click **\"Pop Waitlist\"** to dequeue from the circular buffer. When empty, an **UNDERFLOW** safeguard will alert you.\n\n---\n\n### 4. 🪪 Driver Fleet & Hash Table Lookup\n*Data Structures: Singly Linked List (`Driver* next`) & Hash Table with Chaining*\n\n- **Register a Driver**:\n  1. Enter a Driver Name (e.g., \"Alex Drake\").\n  2. Select their current station location.\n  3. Set available passenger seats (e.g., 4).\n  4. Click **\"Register Driver\"**.\n  5. The driver is appended to the **Singly Linked List** showing pointer arrows `&rarr; next`.\n  6. The driver is simultaneously inserted into the **Hash Table** bucket based on `id % 6`.\n- **$O(1)$ Hash Table Search**:\n  1. Enter a driver's ID in the \"ID to Search\" field (e.g., `101`).\n  2. Click **\"Query O(1)\"**.\n  3. The exact bucket card highlights with a glowing border and displays the driver's profile.\n\n---\n\n### 5. 🚖 Confirm Rides & Binary Search Tree (BST)\n*Data Structures: Doubly Linked List (`prev <-> next`) & Binary Search Tree*\n\n- **Confirm & Match Ride**:\n  - Click **\"Confirm & Match Next Ride\"**.\n  - The engine matches the front passenger with an available driver who has free seats.\n  - The driver's seat count is automatically decremented.\n  - The ride is inserted into the **Doubly Linked List** with bidirectional arrows (`prev` and `next`).\n  - The ride is added to the **Binary Search Tree (BST)** sorted by Ride ID, redrawing the tree branches and updating the in-order traversal list.\n- **Cancel a Ride**: Click the `✕` icon on any active ride card to remove it from both the Doubly Linked List and the BST.\n\n---\n\n### 6. ⏪ Undo Latest Ride Mechanism (LIFO Stack)\n*Data Structures: Stack (`std::stack<UndoAction>`)*\n\n- Each confirmed ride pushes an action frame onto the **Undo Stack** visualizer.\n- Click **\"Undo Latest Ride (Stack Pop)\"**:\n  - Pops the top frame from the stack.\n  - Automatically cancels the active ride from the Doubly Linked List and BST.\n  - Restores the matched driver's available seat count.\n  - Returns the passenger back to the front of the queue so they don't lose their place.\n\n---\n\n### 7. 💻 C++ Backend Source Code Inspector\n- Click the **\"C++ Backend Structs\"** button in the header.\n- An interactive modal drawer opens containing the actual C++ code:\n  - Tab 1: `CityGraph` & `dijkstra()` with `std::priority_queue`.\n  - Tab 2: `CircularWaitlistQueue` with modulo math and overflow protection.\n  - Tab 3: `DriverNode` (Singly LL) & `ActiveRideDoublyList` (Doubly LL with bidirectional deletion).\n  - Tab 4: `DriverHashTable` with separate chaining buckets.\n  - Tab 5: `RideBST` with dynamic node insertion and in-order traversal.\n  - Tab 6: `RideUndoManager` backed by `std::stack`.\n\n---\n\n### 8. 🧲 3D Anti-Gravity Tilt Effects\n- Move your cursor across any UI card or metric counter.\n- Cards dynamically tilt along the X and Y axes and lift towards your eye along the Z-axis (`translateZ(12px)`) with reactive neon drop-shadows.\n","Description":"Created README.md operating manual","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\Kartik Bhosle\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\6976d1a8-fd03-4453-a790-acd552e1861a\\README.md","toolAction":"Writing README file","toolSummary":"Create README.md in artifacts directory"}}]}
